WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:04.899
Last year, for the first time in the history
of my channel, when I did my extended video

00:00:04.899 --> 00:00:10.910
on the editing in Suicide Squad I indulged
in the well worn trope where a reviewer plays

00:00:10.910 --> 00:00:14.969
up needing drugs or alcohol to get through
the process.

00:00:14.969 --> 00:00:19.780
In the script it was supposed to be cooking
oil as a total subversion of the trope, but

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:29.710
at the last minute I decided to use an old
cough syrup bottle instead, instead

00:00:29.710 --> 00:00:32.040
recreating the trope.

00:00:32.040 --> 00:00:38.650
Anyway, let’s talk about the art of storytelling
and The Book of Henry!

00:00:38.650 --> 00:00:50.390
I’m going to warn you, The Book of Henry
is an infectious movie.

00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:55.100
There’s a decent risk that knowing about
this movie will make you insufferable to friends

00:00:55.100 --> 00:01:01.950
and family as you insist on recounting every
mind-numbing twist in excruciating detail.

00:01:01.950 --> 00:01:05.630
The Book of Henry is a case study in what
happens when you spend twenty years writing

00:01:05.630 --> 00:01:08.289
and re-writing the same script.

00:01:08.289 --> 00:01:11.880
Crime novelist Steven Horwitz originally
started shopping the script around in the

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:12.880
mid 90s.

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:16.659
It got some buzz and made the rounds, but
ultimately wasn’t produced.

00:01:16.659 --> 00:01:30.189
Horwitz held on to it and kept making changes
as the years passed and he grew older.

00:01:30.189 --> 00:01:44.130
It’s no wonder, then, that the film is something
of a cross section of two decades of cloying

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:50.209
children’s films and films-about-children,
from Little Man Tate and Radio Flyer, through

00:01:50.209 --> 00:01:54.529
Pay It Forward, to Extremely Loud and Incredibly
Close.

00:01:54.529 --> 00:01:59.139
The end product is, from a critical perspective,
like a sudoku puzzle.

00:01:59.139 --> 00:02:04.759
The failings and poor decisions are interlinked
in a way that explaining one requires understanding

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:07.209
the relative construction of the others.

00:02:07.209 --> 00:02:13.640
There is not a singular problem, but a complex
system of co-dependant problems that are ultimately

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:19.700
rooted in the insurmountable flaw that is
the movie’s basic premise.

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:24.520
This is a movie about a mother, Susan, who
sets out to murder her neighbour, Glen, and

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:29.220
kidnap his step daughter, Christina, following
instructions left behind by her genius son,

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.060
Henry, who just died of brain cancer.

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:36.230
Or it’s about Henry, a kid genius who decides
to kill his neighbour, Glen, but develops

00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:40.430
terminal brain cancer and spends his final
days preparing meticulous instructions so

00:02:40.430 --> 00:02:45.480
his mother, Susan, can carry out the murder
and kidnapping after his death.

00:02:45.480 --> 00:02:49.680
But it’s also a heartwarming, life-affirming
reminder that cynicism weighs us down and

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:53.840
we should be doers, not dreamers, and we should
murder our neighbours, but only if their kids

00:02:53.840 --> 00:03:00.689
are sad, because as we all know, a sad teenager
is an abused teenager.

00:03:00.689 --> 00:03:06.140
So to start this off properly I’m going
to try, as best I can, to simply, factually,

00:03:06.140 --> 00:03:10.390
recount the plot of the film without commentary,
questions, or asides.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:13.329
Alright let’s go.

00:03:13.329 --> 00:03:17.269
Henry and his younger brother Peter get on
the bus while Henry, in voice over, waxes

00:03:17.269 --> 00:03:21.640
philosophic about the nature of man, suggesting
that sometimes people will surprise you, while

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:27.900
the rest of the time people are exactly who
they seem to be, which is to say: cruel.

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:32.209
In class Henry improvises his essay project
in front of the class, mocks another student,

00:03:32.209 --> 00:03:35.709
and tells his teacher that he shouldn’t
be in an advanced class because

00:03:35.709 --> 00:03:42.530
[better for psycho-social development]

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:47.069
While waiting after school Henry buys and
sells stocks over the pay phone until mom,

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:48.969
Susan, pulls up in a beat up car.

00:03:48.969 --> 00:03:53.450
Henry tells her she should buy a new car because
they can afford a new car, but mom says there’s

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:56.010
nothing wrong with the old car.

00:03:56.010 --> 00:04:00.079
At home Peter bugs Henry over the radio until
Henry agrees to let Peter help him with his

00:04:00.079 --> 00:04:05.370
Rube Goldberg machine.

00:04:05.370 --> 00:04:09.010
That night Susan puts Peter into the bath
and then plays video games while Henry does

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:12.349
the family finances, telling mom that she
needs to pay more attention.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:17.510
At bedtime Susan reads the two boys a story
that she wrote, she finds an engineering award

00:04:17.510 --> 00:04:20.600
that Henry won, and Henry gives the medal
to Peter.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:24.480
At breakfast the next morning Henry continues
to bug mom about the finances, trying to teach

00:04:24.480 --> 00:04:28.230
her how to use the computer, and the kids
correct her middle finger posture when she

00:04:28.230 --> 00:04:29.390
flips Henry off.

00:04:29.390 --> 00:04:32.920
They’re called outside by their neighbour
Glen Sickleman, the police commissioner, who

00:04:32.920 --> 00:04:37.530
complains about the leaves on their lawn,
and Susan dots on the neighbour girl, Christina.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.950
At work Susan banters with her co-worker Shiela.

00:04:39.950 --> 00:04:44.930
Her boss, Jon, gives her her paycheck, suggests
she set up direct deposit, and Susan says

00:04:44.930 --> 00:04:50.920
she’ll need to check with Henry.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:54.680
At school Henry asks Christina how she’s
doing, and Peter gets beat up.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.070
The medal that Henry gave Peter gets broken.

00:04:57.070 --> 00:05:02.670
Back home Peter sulks, Henry cheers him up
by doing… this.

00:05:02.670 --> 00:05:05.450
Then Susan comes home with Sheila, the two
get drunk.

00:05:05.450 --> 00:05:10.560
Henry wakes up with a headache, spies on Christina
next door, and ominous music tells us something

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:14.910
is up with Glen.

00:05:14.910 --> 00:05:18.870
The next morning Susan drives the kids and
Christina to school, Henry keeps looking at

00:05:18.870 --> 00:05:23.200
her, on the ride to school and in class, then
he gets up, storms into the principal’s

00:05:23.200 --> 00:05:27.090
office, and says “goddammit Janice, how
much longer does this have to go on?”

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:30.120
They argue about Christina being abused by
Glen.

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:34.030
Goddammit Janice tells him she needs conclusive
evidence, and Henry says he’ll handle it

00:05:34.030 --> 00:05:35.030
alone.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:38.220
The kids get picked up by Susan after school,
and they stop by Sheila’s, and then they

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:42.270
get groceries and see a dude beating on his
girlfriend and Henry wants to get involved

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:45.340
but Susan tells him to mind his own business.

00:05:45.340 --> 00:05:49.560
Henry calls Child Protective Services on Glen,
then while everyone is unpacking groceries

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:55.580
Christina puts a lot of weighty emphasis on
the fact that Glen is her step… father.

00:05:55.580 --> 00:05:59.880
Susan agrees to drive Christina to and from
the upcoming talent show because Glen isn’t

00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:00.880
planning on going.

00:06:00.880 --> 00:06:02.720
Susan tells Henry to be less serious.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:07.380
Henry says he should have gotten involved
in the supermarket and that violence isn’t

00:06:07.380 --> 00:06:08.390
the worst thing.

00:06:08.390 --> 00:06:13.160
CPS checks on Glen and Christina, but the
head of CPS is Glen’s brother, so Henry

00:06:13.160 --> 00:06:18.180
starts driving around town on his bike, making
notes in a red notebook, buys books on crime

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:22.710
scene investigation, and scouts out a gun
store where he overhears some dude trying

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:31.360
to buy an illegal gun by dropping the name
“Dominic.”

00:06:31.360 --> 00:06:35.120
Back home Susan is playing Gears of War and
Henry tells her to shut it off.

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:40.940
At bedtime there’s an argument about who’s
enchilada number one and who’s enchilada

00:06:40.940 --> 00:06:44.480
number two, then Susan sings them a song.

00:06:44.480 --> 00:06:47.950
In the middle of the night Susan is woken
up by Peter screaming because Henry is having

00:06:47.950 --> 00:06:48.950
a seizure.

00:06:48.950 --> 00:06:53.150
He’s rushed to hospital, it’s incurable
brain cancer, and Henry explains his own disease

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:59.130
to the neurosurgeon.

00:06:59.130 --> 00:07:02.280
Sheila visits Henry in the hospital and kisses
him.

00:07:02.280 --> 00:07:03.710
[I am not okay with this]

00:07:03.710 --> 00:07:07.620
Henry and Susan argue about the finances,
Henry tells Peter to make mom read his red

00:07:07.620 --> 00:07:14.270
notebook, Henry and Susan argue about finances
again, then Henry dies in the middle of the

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:15.270
night.

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:18.750
After a few days, Peter comes home, Susan
is making brownies for dinner, and doctor

00:07:18.750 --> 00:07:22.150
hottie comes by for a house call where he
gives Peter his cell number.

00:07:22.150 --> 00:07:27.440
At work Susan is losing her mind, Jon sends
her home and tells her that Henry told him

00:07:27.440 --> 00:07:33.491
via letter that she has six hundred and eighty
thousand dollars in a chequing account, and

00:07:33.491 --> 00:07:35.590
even more in stocks and bonds.

00:07:35.590 --> 00:07:38.141
At home Peter finds Henry’s red notebook,
which

00:07:38.141 --> 00:07:46.180
[I think Henry wants us to kill Glen]

00:07:46.180 --> 00:07:50.400
Susan argues with the book, calls CPS herself,
spys on Glen, and then is told by the book

00:07:50.400 --> 00:07:53.400
to check the safe where she finds tapes and
a walkman.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:57.870
The tapes walk her step by step through withdrawing
money from the bank, avoiding security cameras

00:07:57.870 --> 00:07:59.310
at the gun store, and buying

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:09.370
[stuff]

00:08:09.370 --> 00:08:13.900
Susan then gets a copy of Glen’s signature
which she uses, as per the tapes’ instructions,

00:08:13.900 --> 00:08:16.470
to forge custody papers for Christina.

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:20.370
At the talent show, doctor hottie shows up
because Peter invited him.

00:08:20.370 --> 00:08:26.120
Once the show starts Susan synchronizes her
watch, jumps into her new car that Henry bought,

00:08:26.120 --> 00:08:29.370
drives back to the tree fort, and sets up
to take the shot.

00:08:29.370 --> 00:08:34.670
While she’s aiming, back at talent show
Christina dances a sad dance, and Goddammit

00:08:34.670 --> 00:08:38.280
Janice, seeing the sad dance, goes and makes
a phone call.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:43.180
Back at the treehouse Susan has a brief moment
where she realizes that holy crap she’s

00:08:43.180 --> 00:08:48.010
taking instructions from a tape recorded by
her dead eleven year old and decides not to

00:08:48.010 --> 00:08:52.620
shoot the police commissioner in the head
with a sniper rifle.

00:08:52.620 --> 00:08:53.920
Standing ovation.

00:08:53.920 --> 00:08:58.500
There’s your big Kuleshov Effect moment
of the movie, by the way.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:08:59.500
Glen confronts her

00:08:59.500 --> 00:09:01.870
[what the hell is going on?]

00:09:01.870 --> 00:09:06.210
She threatens him, jumps in her car to get
back to the talent show, Glen goes home, makes

00:09:06.210 --> 00:09:09.030
a phone call, and shoots himself.

00:09:09.030 --> 00:09:13.000
Susan gets back just in time for Peter’s
magic act, where he summons the spirit of

00:09:13.000 --> 00:09:16.360
Henry from his trunk in the form of a fake
snowstorm.

00:09:16.360 --> 00:09:18.230
Christina learns her step father is dead.

00:09:18.230 --> 00:09:22.710
The court gives custody to Susan.

00:09:22.710 --> 00:09:27.300
Susan burns all evidence of her crimes, and
the movie ends with Susan tucking Enchilada

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:31.310
number one and Butterfly number one into bed.

00:09:31.310 --> 00:09:32.940
Credits roll.

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:40.589
[title card: Money Problems, or Lack Thereof]

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:43.950
This might seem like an odd point to start
the actual autopsy with, but let’s talk

00:09:43.950 --> 00:09:46.360
about the money issues in the film.

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:50.700
In the middle of the movie, after Henry dies,
Susan’s boss Jon gives her an envelope,

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:55.070
a letter from Henry, that says she has six
hundred and eighty thousand dollars in the

00:09:55.070 --> 00:09:57.900
bank, and even more in stocks and bonds.

00:09:57.900 --> 00:09:58.900
They’re rich!

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:03.260
Henry has, from beyond the grave, saved the
family from poverty!

00:10:03.260 --> 00:10:07.190
No more will mom be forced to miss bedtime
because she’s working late!

00:10:07.190 --> 00:10:12.300
She can now realize her true passion to be
a children’s picture book author!

00:10:12.300 --> 00:10:13.690
This is a genre touchstone.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:18.180
These sorts of movies about exceptional kids
almost always have some element of overcoming

00:10:18.180 --> 00:10:21.520
or just dealing with financial difficulty,
often poverty.

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:22.589
It’s easy drama.

00:10:22.589 --> 00:10:27.680
Little Man Tate, Radio Flyer, and Pay it Forward
all hit this beat in one way or another.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:31.980
Single mom raising one or two kids at the
edge of financial ruin, working at a diner,

00:10:31.980 --> 00:10:35.810
driving a beat up car, joking with her coworkers
about the hardships of being rich.

00:10:35.810 --> 00:10:37.180
Here’s the problem.

00:10:37.180 --> 00:10:41.970
The family in The Book of Henry is doing fine.

00:10:41.970 --> 00:10:45.000
They’re fine.

00:10:45.000 --> 00:10:51.490
Earlier in the film Jon explicitly points
out that they live in a nice neighbourhood.

00:10:51.490 --> 00:10:59.860
I mean, heck, they live right next door to
the police commissioner.

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:02.180
Henry buys and sells stocks over the phone.

00:11:02.180 --> 00:11:07.050
Susan only seems to work part time, and it’s
never a worry about how they’ll pay the

00:11:07.050 --> 00:11:08.050
bills.

00:11:08.050 --> 00:11:13.590
Henry has brain surgery and no one is concerned
with how they’re going to pay for it.

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:18.240
Hell, our first introduction to Susan is contextualized
by money.

00:11:18.240 --> 00:11:26.350
[I wish you’d get a new car, it’s not
like you can’t afford it.]

00:11:26.350 --> 00:11:30.480
These beats comes up several times, where
Henry explicitly points out that they can

00:11:30.480 --> 00:11:33.610
afford stuff, and she never disagrees.

00:11:33.610 --> 00:11:35.550
In fact the opposite.

00:11:35.550 --> 00:11:42.800
She chides him and says that being able to
afford it isn’t the point.

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:47.170
He also tells Susan that she can afford to
quit her job at the diner and focus on writing.

00:11:47.170 --> 00:11:51.250
He’s constantly telling her to pay attention
to the financial statements and investment

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:56.850
reports and bank statements, but the reveal
that they have all this money doesn’t really

00:11:56.850 --> 00:12:00.470
mean anything from a story standpoint.

00:12:00.470 --> 00:12:07.700
This scene works just as well if that line
is cut, because the actually important line

00:12:07.700 --> 00:12:10.250
is this.

00:12:10.250 --> 00:12:15.030
[no, you’re not fine]

00:12:15.030 --> 00:12:21.480
Hell, if anything the scene accidentally implies
that she was pretending to be in a paycheck-to-paycheck

00:12:21.480 --> 00:12:27.430
situation and that Henry has outed her wealth
to her friends and coworkers, humiliating

00:12:27.430 --> 00:12:28.430
her.

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:33.710
Now, the fact that the family isn’t living
in poverty isn’t itself a problem.

00:12:33.710 --> 00:12:38.350
Just because it’s a staple of the genre
doesn’t make it mandatory, we don’t need

00:12:38.350 --> 00:12:41.800
that for it to be effective or complete.

00:12:41.800 --> 00:12:46.510
But, as you can see from all these examples,
the issue of money comes up often.

00:12:46.510 --> 00:12:51.770
It is not an unstated dimension or a background
detail.

00:12:51.770 --> 00:12:56.960
The weird thing is that it actively works
against the theme of the film.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:13:00.570
This is a rare film that opens with a thesis
statement.

00:13:00.570 --> 00:13:05.029
Henry’s improvised essay in front of the
class is a direct philosophical statement

00:13:05.029 --> 00:13:10.360
aimed at the audience, and in Henry’s little
Tyler Durden Jr. screed he explicitly denounces

00:13:10.360 --> 00:13:22.680
the exact sort of obsession over money that
he then spends the rest of his life pursuing.

00:13:22.680 --> 00:13:30.580
[montage]

00:13:30.580 --> 00:14:05.630
Ha ha ha, it’s a bad movie.

00:14:05.630 --> 00:14:17.410
[title card: Henry’s Plan is Bad and Stupid]

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:20.390
Henry’s plan is bad and stupid.

00:14:20.390 --> 00:14:25.380
It’s exactly the kind of bad and stupid
Rube Goldberg Loony Tunes assassination plan

00:14:25.380 --> 00:14:28.440
that you’d expect to be cooked up by a dumb
eleven year old.

00:14:28.440 --> 00:14:32.580
That in and of itself could be something fun
and interesting if it weren’t offensively

00:14:32.580 --> 00:14:37.580
paired with the incredibly heavy subject of
rescuing someone from being raped, and the

00:14:37.580 --> 00:14:43.660
fact that an adult follows the plan almost
to its completion before having a minor epiphany

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:48.430
that maybe murder isn’t actually the answer.

00:14:48.430 --> 00:14:53.670
This is all paralleled by constant assertions
that Henry is, in fact, a super mega genius

00:14:53.670 --> 00:14:55.900
who thinks of everything.

00:14:55.900 --> 00:15:00.760
So for fun let’s point out all the flaws
in this flawless plan.

00:15:00.760 --> 00:15:05.380
From the start I need to emphasize that this
is a plan to kill Glen.

00:15:05.380 --> 00:15:11.290
It’s also, theoretically, a plan to rescue
Christina from abuse, but first and foremost

00:15:11.290 --> 00:15:13.390
it’s a plan to kill Glen.

00:15:13.390 --> 00:15:17.810
We’ll talk about this in more detail later,
but so little of the plan revolves around

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:24.071
or involves Christina that, really, rescuing
Christina is just the justification for a

00:15:24.071 --> 00:15:25.440
plot to kill Glen.

00:15:25.440 --> 00:15:30.270
You could swap the motive for anything else
and very, very little would change.

00:15:30.270 --> 00:15:34.350
The first thing to highlight is that most
of this plan is drafted before Henry knows

00:15:34.350 --> 00:15:39.310
he’s going to die, meaning that he was,
in concept at least, intending to somehow

00:15:39.310 --> 00:15:41.190
follow through with these steps.

00:15:41.190 --> 00:15:46.870
This is a big screenwriting problem because
the writer already knows Henry dies before

00:15:46.870 --> 00:15:52.890
the character knows, so the plan is accidentally
built around the convenience that Henry doesn’t

00:15:52.890 --> 00:15:54.300
need to execute the plan.

00:15:54.300 --> 00:15:59.310
A lot of the smaller details of the plan are
suggested as part of various preparation montages,

00:15:59.310 --> 00:16:04.010
and illustrations from Henry’s notebook,
which we fortunately get in close up during

00:16:04.010 --> 00:16:05.649
the opening credits.

00:16:05.649 --> 00:16:10.330
Despite all the attention given to minute
details like the type of bullet used and clearing

00:16:10.330 --> 00:16:14.720
all the branches, the basic concept of the
plan is really simple.

00:16:14.720 --> 00:16:19.360
Get a gun, lure Glen out into the woods, and
shoot him in the head from the comfort of

00:16:19.360 --> 00:16:20.660
the playhouse.

00:16:20.660 --> 00:16:24.900
After that, as per the illustration, the body
falls into the river, and then the gun gets

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:26.990
thrown off the bridge into the lake.

00:16:26.990 --> 00:16:31.770
The biggest hole here is that every version
of the plan revolves around shooting Glen.

00:16:31.770 --> 00:16:35.720
Henry explicitly scopes out the gun store,
and gets the name Dominic, at a point when

00:16:35.720 --> 00:16:38.520
he assumes the headaches are just stress.

00:16:38.520 --> 00:16:43.600
Except if we take the cancer out of the equation
this is where the plan ends, because even

00:16:43.600 --> 00:16:48.290
with the whole Dominic thing, even the gun
store owner willing to bend the paperwork

00:16:48.290 --> 00:16:53.730
rules for cash isn’t going to sell a sniper
rifle to an 11 year old.

00:16:53.730 --> 00:16:55.399
The next biggest hole is the body.

00:16:55.399 --> 00:17:01.690
Again, the scriptwriter knows that neither
Henry nor Susan will actually kill Glen, so

00:17:01.690 --> 00:17:06.339
they don’t bother to sweat the details,
which leaves us with Henry intending for Glen’s

00:17:06.339 --> 00:17:10.919
body to fall into the river, and by river
I mean creek.

00:17:10.919 --> 00:17:11.919
This creek.

00:17:11.919 --> 00:17:16.499
Now, to me, someone who watches way too many
police procedurals, this isn’t so much a

00:17:16.499 --> 00:17:22.490
hide a body creek as much as it’s a hey
boss, we found-a-body down by the creek creek.

00:17:22.490 --> 00:17:27.409
So Henry’s master plan to kill Glen is really
just kill him in your own backyard and hope

00:17:27.409 --> 00:17:29.399
no one suspects his neighbours.

00:17:29.399 --> 00:17:34.289
Once we move away from the concept, into the
weeds of the details, is when the plan really

00:17:34.289 --> 00:17:36.909
falls apart into needless complexity.

00:17:36.909 --> 00:17:43.460
The hilarious thing is that Henry repeatedly
stresses the need to plan for every contingency,

00:17:43.460 --> 00:17:55.279
but then fails to plan for even basic contingencies.

00:17:55.279 --> 00:18:00.200
The most glaring hole is in step one: lure
Glen out into the woods.

00:18:00.200 --> 00:18:04.440
Apparently the best idea the authors could
think of was making noise with a walkie-talkie

00:18:04.440 --> 00:18:05.620
strapped to an amplifier.

00:18:05.620 --> 00:18:07.519
Seriously, that’s the plan.

00:18:07.519 --> 00:18:12.559
Susan whistles into a walkie-talkie and this
noise is suspicious enough that Glen gets

00:18:12.559 --> 00:18:15.500
his gun before going to investigate.

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:20.100
It also needs to be stressed here that everything
in the details of the plan hinges on a very

00:18:20.100 --> 00:18:26.259
tight schedule, as Susan needs to be seen
at the talent show, get home, kill Glen, dispose

00:18:26.259 --> 00:18:30.720
of the evidence, and get back before anyone
notices that she’s gone.

00:18:30.720 --> 00:18:36.619
So plan for every contingency, but the whole
thing falls apart if Glen watches a movie,

00:18:36.619 --> 00:18:43.470
gets a phone call, takes a shower, or does
literally anything that would prevent or delay

00:18:43.470 --> 00:18:48.279
hearing the noise that’s supposed to lure
him to his death.

00:18:48.279 --> 00:18:52.350
A great problem with the plan is that Henry,
via tape, has Susan run around to a bunch

00:18:52.350 --> 00:18:56.289
of different ATMs to withdraw a few hundred
dollars in cash at each.

00:18:56.289 --> 00:19:00.769
He says that this is to avoid suspicion and
get around withdrawal limits, but hitting

00:19:00.769 --> 00:19:06.450
up a bunch of different ATMs in a short span
of time is exactly the kind of behaviour that’s

00:19:06.450 --> 00:19:08.059
going to draw suspicion.

00:19:08.059 --> 00:19:10.790
Also, sweetie, you’re rich.

00:19:10.790 --> 00:19:14.119
You have well over a million dollars in investments
and cash.

00:19:14.119 --> 00:19:16.999
Just go to the bank and take out the money
like a normal person.

00:19:16.999 --> 00:19:18.590
It’s not even that much.

00:19:18.590 --> 00:19:23.619
She only takes out $1500, which is, one, not
enough to buy the hardware she gets from the

00:19:23.619 --> 00:19:28.150
gun store, and two, not a suspicious amount
of money.

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:30.950
That’s buying crap off Kijiji money.

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:34.230
Oh, also, last note here, just because it’s
hilarious

00:19:34.230 --> 00:19:37.200
[plywood is slightly thicker than the human
skull]

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:42.590
This isn’t a flaw in the plan, it’s just
a really dumb line, one that they were so

00:19:42.590 --> 00:19:45.480
proud of they made sure to include it in the
title cards.

00:19:45.480 --> 00:19:51.440
You’re shooting 30 caliber frangible bullets,
his skull isn’t going to be the problem

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:58.139
here.

00:19:58.139 --> 00:20:06.340
[Henry is a Dick]

00:20:06.340 --> 00:20:08.720
[emotional intelligence]

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:15.649
That’s a mind-boggling statement that just
says so much about the way Colin Trevorrow

00:20:15.649 --> 00:20:16.940
sees the world.

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:21.019
Henry is the least emotionally aware person
in the film.

00:20:21.019 --> 00:20:22.690
He’s actually a huge prick.

00:20:22.690 --> 00:20:27.830
I mean, his first actual scene is him dunking
on this kid, Tommy, for not being grown up

00:20:27.830 --> 00:20:37.220
enough for Henry’s tastes.

00:20:37.220 --> 00:20:41.869
Rather than being a kid who has been unfairly
forced to grow up by a negligent, absent,

00:20:41.869 --> 00:20:46.220
or addicted parent, Henry has self-appointed
himself to be the adult.

00:20:46.220 --> 00:20:49.940
This is tied to the movie’s characterization
of Susan, which we’ll get to shortly, but

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:54.840
there’s no indication that things wouldn’t
get done without Henry around.

00:20:54.840 --> 00:21:00.139
Henry is the kind of person who self-martyrs
because they see other people as incompetent.

00:21:00.139 --> 00:21:04.549
The film also tries to play Henry two ways,
and can’t decide which is which.

00:21:04.549 --> 00:21:08.179
Susan tells him to spend less time focused
on his school work, and there’s a whole

00:21:08.179 --> 00:21:13.080
half a scene about all the awards that Henry
wins for extracurricular activities.

00:21:13.080 --> 00:21:17.330
So on one hand we’re supposed to think about
Henry as the kind of keener who does all the

00:21:17.330 --> 00:21:18.989
bonus work, and then some.

00:21:18.989 --> 00:21:23.999
But then in that first scene, the Tyler Durden
Jr. speech, Henry is supposed to be reading

00:21:23.999 --> 00:21:28.539
an essay that he’s written, except he’s
clearly just improvising.

00:21:28.539 --> 00:21:30.169
He didn’t do the work at all.

00:21:30.169 --> 00:21:35.059
So there’s the other implication, that he’s
a waster, the smart kid who thinks the work

00:21:35.059 --> 00:21:36.190
is beneath him.

00:21:36.190 --> 00:21:39.840
To cap it off, he mocks his teacher.

00:21:39.840 --> 00:21:47.340
[psycho social development]

00:21:47.340 --> 00:21:50.029
This whole moment is demonstrably false.

00:21:50.029 --> 00:21:55.779
Every shot of him in class, he looks absurdly
bored, and he doesn’t have friends.

00:21:55.779 --> 00:22:00.140
Henry spends his lunch playing checkers in
the cafeteria with the lunch lady, a scene

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:04.490
where he does the whole movie smart person
shorthand of beating the game in one move

00:22:04.490 --> 00:22:09.899
just to show that he was in control all along,
which is, again, a huge dick move.

00:22:09.899 --> 00:22:12.910
It’s also incredibly hack on the part of
the screenwriter.

00:22:12.910 --> 00:22:17.350
Like, this is just the worst kind of lazy
trope.

00:22:17.350 --> 00:22:21.739
Oh, and we can’t forget the scene where
Henry, cop on the edge, bursts into Goddammit

00:22:21.739 --> 00:22:29.480
Janice’s office and threatens to turn in
his badge.

00:22:29.480 --> 00:22:33.169
Henry also gets really judgy about Susan playing
video games.

00:22:33.169 --> 00:22:37.629
He mocks the way that she plays, and polices
what she does with her free time.

00:22:37.629 --> 00:22:43.830
He’s actually really, really condescending
to Susan.

00:22:43.830 --> 00:23:15.100
[montage of times Henry is 
a dick, don’t forget “violence isn’t

00:23:15.100 --> 00:23:21.090
the worst thing”]

00:23:21.090 --> 00:23:43.830
[you’re doing it wrong]

00:23:43.830 --> 00:23:49.510
What?

00:23:49.510 --> 00:23:57.010
No, no she’s not.

00:23:57.010 --> 00:24:03.090
The middle finger is a gesture with a high
degree of plasticity.

00:24:03.090 --> 00:24:05.429
You have your classic style

00:24:05.429 --> 00:24:07.330
Detroit style

00:24:07.330 --> 00:24:08.539
The web shooters

00:24:08.539 --> 00:24:11.559
And, of course, The Logan.

00:24:11.559 --> 00:24:13.729
There’s nothing wrong with her form.

00:24:13.729 --> 00:24:18.590
You’re not being accurate, you’re just
being a dick, and you deserve to get flipped

00:24:18.590 --> 00:24:19.590
off.

00:24:19.590 --> 00:24:24.519
Oh, and how can we forget the part where Henry
cooks up a plot to murder his neighbour.

00:24:24.519 --> 00:24:27.549
[high degree of emotional intelligence]

00:24:27.549 --> 00:24:34.419
[Title card: Susan is Negligent, But Not Really]

00:24:34.419 --> 00:24:39.389
How the film characterizes Susan is, for me
at least, the weirdest part of the way the

00:24:39.389 --> 00:24:41.009
movie is constructed.

00:24:41.009 --> 00:24:45.169
All of the previous stuff about the way that
Henry interacts with her, there’s a dissonance

00:24:45.169 --> 00:24:49.799
in that the movie still seems to think that
Henry is always correct.

00:24:49.799 --> 00:24:55.179
He’s an asshole genius as directed by someone
who doesn’t think that he’s an asshole.

00:24:55.179 --> 00:25:00.279
The byproduct of this is that every time Henry
is a judgy prick to his mom, the framing and

00:25:00.279 --> 00:25:07.080
consequences always skew towards Henry.

00:25:07.080 --> 00:25:11.340
Even in the big death bed argument, where
Henry tells Susan to focus on the finances,

00:25:11.340 --> 00:25:17.309
and Susan says it doesn’t matter, this isn’t
ultimately framed as Henry’s failure, his

00:25:17.309 --> 00:25:22.499
fear in the face of mortality, but instead
as Susan’s weakness.

00:25:22.499 --> 00:25:26.850
Within the framework of the story this isn’t
Henry in denial, it’s Henry trying to tie

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:36.820
up loose ends because he’s the adult in
the relationship.

00:25:36.820 --> 00:25:41.779
Henry’s plans and behaviour never actually
fail or fall apart.

00:25:41.779 --> 00:25:46.970
The closest it gets is when Susan decides
not to shoot Glen, but nothing actually comes

00:25:46.970 --> 00:25:50.389
from that because Glen kills himself a few
minutes later.

00:25:50.389 --> 00:25:54.489
This moment, right here, where Susan goes
off-script, this is where the story finally

00:25:54.489 --> 00:25:59.340
picks up, where the stakes are raised, but
then it just ends and everything else goes

00:25:59.340 --> 00:26:02.860
according to Henry’s plan with no fallout
whatsoever.

00:26:02.860 --> 00:26:07.710
So this creates a dynamic where the film is
constantly suggesting things about Susan that

00:26:07.710 --> 00:26:11.370
aren’t actually true if you think about
it for more than a minute.

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:16.279
From a structural standpoint, the main problem
with Susan is that she has no arc as a character

00:26:16.279 --> 00:26:21.999
because none of her real problems are addressed,
and the problem that is addressed was never

00:26:21.999 --> 00:26:23.630
truly a problem.

00:26:23.630 --> 00:26:25.749
Susan’s deal is that she’s complacent.

00:26:25.749 --> 00:26:29.960
She’s comfortable, she’s safe, she’s
financially secure, and she’s just kind

00:26:29.960 --> 00:26:34.700
of coasting through life, raising her two
kids, enjoying being in a place where nothing

00:26:34.700 --> 00:26:37.440
requires her constant, unbroken attention.

00:26:37.440 --> 00:26:41.370
She has plenty of time to bond with her kids,
drive them to and from school, take them to

00:26:41.370 --> 00:26:44.190
events, make them breakfast, and pack their
lunches.

00:26:44.190 --> 00:26:53.429
Now, she does have a bad case of Cool Mom,
she’s overly codependent on Henry, and she

00:26:53.429 --> 00:26:59.519
has no real ambitions, but the family is,
objectively, doing fine.

00:26:59.519 --> 00:27:04.129
She’s not super keyed into the family finances
because she doesn’t need to be.

00:27:04.129 --> 00:27:08.710
That’s the luxury of being well off, regardless
of Henry’s involvement.

00:27:08.710 --> 00:27:11.970
This goes back to everything we talked about
with the money sub-plot.

00:27:11.970 --> 00:27:16.409
Susan ostensibly doesn’t know about all
the money that Henry has made on the stock

00:27:16.409 --> 00:27:19.510
market, but it doesn’t seem to bother Susan
at all.

00:27:19.510 --> 00:27:24.830
As far as she’s concerned the family is
financially secure off whatever income she

00:27:24.830 --> 00:27:29.440
has, and nothing suggests that if it weren’t
for Henry they’d be screwed.

00:27:29.440 --> 00:27:36.389
I mean, presumably they survived just fine
before Henry started trading stocks, right?

00:27:36.389 --> 00:27:42.109
Being a Cool Mom is a character flaw, in the
absolute sense, but there’s no stakes to

00:27:42.109 --> 00:27:43.109
it.

00:27:43.109 --> 00:27:44.109
It’s not causing problems.

00:27:44.109 --> 00:27:50.429
Cool Mom is a surface level problem, a behavioural
problem, that makes other problems worse.

00:27:50.429 --> 00:27:55.940
Being a cool mom didn’t lead to Henry’s
rapid onset terminal brain cancer being overlooked.

00:27:55.940 --> 00:28:00.789
It’s only kind of a problem for one scene
after Henry’s death where she has a very

00:28:00.789 --> 00:28:04.609
justifiable, but temporary, breakdown.

00:28:04.609 --> 00:28:08.950
Nothing that goes wrong in the second half
of the movie has anything to do with things

00:28:08.950 --> 00:28:14.099
she was failing to do when Henry was alive,
things that Henry was doing that she, the

00:28:14.099 --> 00:28:16.490
parent, should have been doing instead.

00:28:16.490 --> 00:28:20.940
The thing that the movie chooses to use as
synecdoche for Susan’s negligence is that

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:26.529
she plays video games and has one girls night
with Sheila, but none of this is ever at the

00:28:26.529 --> 00:28:27.840
cost of anything.

00:28:27.840 --> 00:28:31.749
She’s not playing games instead of getting
groceries or making breakfast.

00:28:31.749 --> 00:28:36.440
She’s not playing games instead of going
to work (which would still be pointless since

00:28:36.440 --> 00:28:39.950
the movie has explicitly told us that she
doesn’t need to go to work in the first

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:40.950
place.)

00:28:40.950 --> 00:28:44.779
She’s not drinking instead of helping with
homework, or getting drunk in the middle of

00:28:44.779 --> 00:28:48.090
the day, or inviting Sheila over six nights
a week.

00:28:48.090 --> 00:28:51.019
She’s just playing games, sometimes.

00:28:51.019 --> 00:28:52.580
[turn it off]

00:28:52.580 --> 00:28:58.960
Yeah, the movie is super judgemental about
Susan not spending literally 100% of her time

00:28:58.960 --> 00:29:03.100
focused on her kids.

00:29:03.100 --> 00:29:08.470
So as a character arc she kind of learns at
the very end to see her genius dead kid as

00:29:08.470 --> 00:29:13.119
the kid he was and not the small adult he
acted like.

00:29:13.119 --> 00:29:17.090
But he’s already dead, so it’s not like
that changes anything.

00:29:17.090 --> 00:29:19.899
Aside from that she’s just kind of complacent.

00:29:19.899 --> 00:29:25.279
Her sub-plot about wanting to be a picture
book author is, well, it’s disingenuous

00:29:25.279 --> 00:29:27.149
to even call it a sub-plot.

00:29:27.149 --> 00:29:32.590
It’s not Susan’s ambition so much as it’s
the one of her three hobbies that the movie

00:29:32.590 --> 00:29:34.440
approves of.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:39.980
[Title card: Christina is a Lamp]

00:29:39.980 --> 00:29:41.399
Alright, this one is the heavy one.

00:29:41.399 --> 00:29:46.090
Christina’s involvement in the film is a
textbook example of a prop character.

00:29:46.090 --> 00:29:50.259
You could replace her with just about anything
and the plot would still work.

00:29:50.259 --> 00:29:55.840
She could be a sack of jewels that Glen has
stolen and very little would actually change.

00:29:55.840 --> 00:30:01.090
The plot to kill Glen is supposedly motivated
by the desire to save Christina from abuse,

00:30:01.090 --> 00:30:04.440
but Christina isn’t involved in it at all.

00:30:04.440 --> 00:30:07.580
Her agency in all of this is utterly absent.

00:30:07.580 --> 00:30:12.489
This is why it’s really just a plot to kill
Glen, not a plot to save Christina.

00:30:12.489 --> 00:30:17.799
The whole story started from the idea of a
genus kid planning the perfect crime, and

00:30:17.799 --> 00:30:22.399
the dudes who made it were so busy high-fiving
each other for their wit that they didn’t

00:30:22.399 --> 00:30:28.309
bother to ask if a story about an eleven year
old being raped by her step dad was the best

00:30:28.309 --> 00:30:29.669
vehicle for that.

00:30:29.669 --> 00:30:35.149
[bts tonal tightrope clip]

00:30:35.149 --> 00:30:49.109
Jesus, they 
think they nailed it.

00:30:49.109 --> 00:30:54.809
Anyway, Christina only has a half dozen lines
in the entire movie, and no one bothers to

00:30:54.809 --> 00:30:59.850
even try and empower her, only taking the
time to make a few anonymous phone calls before

00:30:59.850 --> 00:31:01.029
giving up.

00:31:01.029 --> 00:31:06.259
Yet we’re expected to believe that she’s
so close with the Carpenter family that she

00:31:06.259 --> 00:31:11.849
has fancy personal handshakes with Susan and
slots effortlessly into the hole Henry left

00:31:11.849 --> 00:31:13.039
behind.

00:31:13.039 --> 00:31:15.440
[disgusted face]

00:31:15.440 --> 00:31:18.999
From a storytelling standpoint there’s a
whole host of problems with how this entire

00:31:18.999 --> 00:31:20.139
plot unfolds.

00:31:20.139 --> 00:31:24.590
We, the audience, first get an inkling that
something’s wrong when Henry notices that

00:31:24.590 --> 00:31:27.309
Christina is sad on the playground.

00:31:27.309 --> 00:31:33.369
That night, by happenstance, Henry is looking
out his bedroom window when he sees, presumably,

00:31:33.369 --> 00:31:35.489
Christina being abused by Glen.

00:31:35.489 --> 00:31:39.489
Now, from our perspective this is the inciting
moment.

00:31:39.489 --> 00:31:44.529
Henry has discovered a crime, and is now going
to start trying to correct it.

00:31:44.529 --> 00:31:49.460
The next morning he tries, lightly, to prod
Christina in the car, and then, in class,

00:31:49.460 --> 00:31:53.770
when she isn’t interested in a donut, he
gets up, storms into the principal’s office

00:31:53.770 --> 00:31:54.770
and

00:31:54.770 --> 00:31:57.889
[goddammit Janice]

00:31:57.889 --> 00:31:59.899
This is complete whiplash.

00:31:59.899 --> 00:32:05.139
We’re twenty minutes into the movie and
all of a sudden this isn’t something new,

00:32:05.139 --> 00:32:09.009
this is an ongoing thing that he has tried
to report before?

00:32:09.009 --> 00:32:14.139
He’s dumping all this new information that
contradicts what we were just told.

00:32:14.139 --> 00:32:18.649
Everything about the framing, music, and acting
in the previous scenes indicate that this

00:32:18.649 --> 00:32:23.190
is new, not some long running thing that Henry
has been dealing with.

00:32:23.190 --> 00:32:27.759
In the first scene with all the key players
nothing about this plot is indicated beyond

00:32:27.759 --> 00:32:31.989
the fact that the kids don’t particularly
like Glen.

00:32:31.989 --> 00:32:35.679
Also in one of the weirdest creative decisions,
even though Henry says she’s been coming

00:32:35.679 --> 00:32:39.600
to school with bruises, the hacks that went
with this visual shorthand [Henry winning

00:32:39.600 --> 00:32:41.669
a game in one move to show he’s smart] decided
that they weren’t going to with a visible

00:32:41.669 --> 00:32:42.669
bruise.

00:32:42.669 --> 00:32:44.119
It’s weird.

00:32:44.119 --> 00:32:46.299
Why wouldn’t you show us that?

00:32:46.299 --> 00:32:49.450
It’s the easiest thing to show.

00:32:49.450 --> 00:32:56.519
Then goddammit Janice says she needs concrete
proof, but bruises weren’t enough, I guess?

00:32:56.519 --> 00:33:01.379
All of this repeats again at the end, when
Susan makes all the same claims, that she

00:33:01.379 --> 00:33:07.559
apparently has all this information stashed
away, but we’ve never seen any of it, which

00:33:07.559 --> 00:33:14.169
is just asinine in a story that’s literally
focused on planning and details.

00:33:14.169 --> 00:33:19.739
We have the process Henry used to partially
forge custody papers, but zilch about gathering

00:33:19.739 --> 00:33:24.380
actual evidence against Glen.

00:33:24.380 --> 00:33:28.729
The movie has this really weird aversion to
making any of the characters actually flawed

00:33:28.729 --> 00:33:30.519
or negligent in some way.

00:33:30.519 --> 00:33:36.309
If the filmmakers gave Christina visible bruises
that they show us, well, suddenly every character

00:33:36.309 --> 00:33:42.480
who can see those bruises, including Susan,
teachers, and goddammit Janice, are accountable.

00:33:42.480 --> 00:33:47.960
Their failure to react or willingness to overlook
that information is damning, and makes them

00:33:47.960 --> 00:33:50.369
complicit in Glen’s abuse.

00:33:50.369 --> 00:33:53.519
The movie goes to weird lengths to give everyone
an out.

00:33:53.519 --> 00:33:58.639
No one makes actual, awful mistakes, they
just make implied mistakes.

00:33:58.639 --> 00:34:03.240
I mean, that’s why they’re even doing
this flimsy due diligence in the first place.

00:34:03.240 --> 00:34:07.549
It’s all just shoddy convenience so that
Henry can be blameless when he decides to

00:34:07.549 --> 00:34:13.549
plan to kill Glen, make it look like there
was no other option, instead of implying that

00:34:13.549 --> 00:34:19.570
maybe Henry was actually just a little too
eager to kill Mister Sickelman next door.

00:34:19.570 --> 00:34:24.970
[Title card: Henry is Jesus]

00:34:24.970 --> 00:34:29.919
Easily one of the strangest elements of the
movie is the weird, mangled Jesus metaphor.

00:34:29.919 --> 00:34:33.619
First of all, the movie is called The Book
of Henry, an aesthetic call to the naming

00:34:33.619 --> 00:34:34.809
of biblical books.

00:34:34.809 --> 00:34:39.619
The Book of Matthew, The Book of John, The
Book of Jeremiah, Job, Ezra, Ezekiel, Mark,

00:34:39.619 --> 00:34:41.609
Luke, Esther, Ruth, Malachi.

00:34:41.609 --> 00:34:45.569
Second, Henry’s death is an aesthetic call
to pieta sculpture.

00:34:45.569 --> 00:34:51.369
The thinking man’s Jesus imagery, for when
a crucifixion pose would be too crass.

00:34:51.369 --> 00:34:56.300
Third, Henry’s number one disciple, the
one who first takes his words and convinces

00:34:56.300 --> 00:34:59.020
others of their importance, is named Peter.

00:34:59.020 --> 00:35:04.240
Fourth, the family name is Carpenter, which
is only about one step down from when Lost

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:07.369
had a metaphysical guide named Christian Shepherd.

00:35:07.369 --> 00:35:12.289
Fifth, at the very end Susan reads her new
story about how a flower dies and fertilizes

00:35:12.289 --> 00:35:17.650
a garden with its body, which is just a muddy
repaint of the Christian belief that Jesus

00:35:17.650 --> 00:35:24.539
died to exalt mankind, but is also clearly
intended as a metaphor for Henry who died…

00:35:24.539 --> 00:35:26.630
but made things better in the process?

00:35:26.630 --> 00:35:33.029
Okay, so, the movie thinks that its message
is about apathy.

00:35:33.029 --> 00:35:38.299
[Henry clip]

00:35:38.299 --> 00:35:43.720
So the takeaway here is that the death of
Henry is what taught Susan to be less apathetic

00:35:43.720 --> 00:35:47.740
and to reach out and help others, that she
went from being unwilling to intervene in

00:35:47.740 --> 00:35:53.089
the grocery store to willing to kill Glen
and forge documents in order to save Christina.

00:35:53.089 --> 00:35:58.299
But she didn’t kill Glen, and, in fact,
once she learns about Christina’s situation,

00:35:58.299 --> 00:36:02.880
especially once she sees it for herself, she’s
anything but apathetic.

00:36:02.880 --> 00:36:07.220
Sure, the things that she tries are largely
ineffective and she quickly latches on to

00:36:07.220 --> 00:36:12.520
the murder plot, but she doesn’t need any
persuading to care.

00:36:12.520 --> 00:36:17.329
Even in the grocery store, the problem isn’t
apathy, it’s feeling powerless.

00:36:17.329 --> 00:36:21.349
She’s worried that if she intervenes it
will make things worse.

00:36:21.349 --> 00:36:24.520
This is not the moral failing she actually
overcomes.

00:36:24.520 --> 00:36:29.410
It’s never even implied that killing Glen
might have unforeseen negative outcomes.

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:34.260
Oh, and if you want an actual example of a
bad deus ex machina ending, it would be when

00:36:34.260 --> 00:36:39.859
principle Goddammit Janice decides, completely
independent of the actions of any of the characters,

00:36:39.859 --> 00:36:45.290
to finally take do her job in a way that neatly
resolves all conflicts while absolving the

00:36:45.290 --> 00:36:47.950
central characters for any of their actions.

00:36:47.950 --> 00:36:52.240
Susan gets away with attempted murder because
Glen kills himself before he can tell anyone

00:36:52.240 --> 00:36:53.240
what she did.

00:36:53.240 --> 00:36:57.950
[Title card: A Lesson in Imagery]

00:36:57.950 --> 00:37:05.440
There’s so, so much more we could talk about,
but to wrap this up I want to talk about one

00:37:05.440 --> 00:37:06.700
scene in particular.

00:37:06.700 --> 00:37:12.250
My favourite scene in the whole movie for
how utterly absurd it is at every level of

00:37:12.250 --> 00:37:13.250
conception.

00:37:13.250 --> 00:37:17.500
For Peter’s act in the talent show he pushes
out a large chest and says that he will make

00:37:17.500 --> 00:37:18.829
his brother Henry reappear.

00:37:18.829 --> 00:37:24.700
[When I’m done he’s going to be here right
among all of us]

00:37:24.700 --> 00:37:28.720
Now, there’s a critical detail from earlier
that needs to be mentioned.

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:33.020
After Henry’s death there’s no funeral
scene, not even in montage.

00:37:33.020 --> 00:37:38.790
This is important because this moment is supposed
to create some uncertainty.

00:37:38.790 --> 00:37:45.490
Did Henry, genus child and Jesus metaphor,
in fact fake his own death and will now pop

00:37:45.490 --> 00:37:47.450
out to the delight of all?

00:37:47.450 --> 00:37:48.779
Who knows!

00:37:48.779 --> 00:37:53.950
So Peter opens the chest, kicks the chest
and it starts spraying fake snow out over

00:37:53.950 --> 00:37:56.040
the audience.

00:37:56.040 --> 00:37:59.609
Just, everywhere.

00:37:59.609 --> 00:38:02.760
Then everyone gets up, standing ovation.

00:38:02.760 --> 00:38:07.010
Peter and Susan hug.

00:38:07.010 --> 00:38:12.799
So, this scene.

00:38:12.799 --> 00:38:14.010
This scene.

00:38:14.010 --> 00:38:15.220
Alright.

00:38:15.220 --> 00:38:19.079
What’s going on here, what they’re trying
to do.

00:38:19.079 --> 00:38:23.660
This is, obviously, a callback to the mountain
climbing nonsense from earlier.

00:38:23.660 --> 00:38:29.599
Which no one in the crowd would be familiar
with, so this is literally just a chest that

00:38:29.599 --> 00:38:31.829
spits out fake snow.

00:38:31.829 --> 00:38:37.670
Metaphorically, this is the Book of Henry’s
version of the baptism of fire inside its

00:38:37.670 --> 00:38:39.300
mangled Jesus metaphor.

00:38:39.300 --> 00:38:45.270
Peter, chief apostle, has preached to the
crowd the whimsy of Henry, and his spirit

00:38:45.270 --> 00:38:47.410
has descended upon them.

00:38:47.410 --> 00:38:50.520
But you know what else it means, visually?

00:38:50.520 --> 00:38:55.230
It means that Peter has promised the crowd
that his brother Henry would be physically

00:38:55.230 --> 00:38:57.270
right here among them.

00:38:57.270 --> 00:39:01.390
And then he sprays them with a dry, white
powder.

00:39:01.390 --> 00:39:07.660
The visual implication, however unintended,
is that Peter scatters Henry’s ashes over

00:39:07.660 --> 00:39:09.940
the crowd.

00:39:09.940 --> 00:39:11.650
Standing ovation.

